This is Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedantam. In 1759, the French writer and philosopher Voltaire published a satirical novel about a young man and his tutor. Pangloss, the tutor, instructs the student, Candide, that they live in, quote, the best of all possible worlds. As the story unfolds, Candide witnesses war, disease, natural disasters, torture, and death. Pangloss encounters many of these tragedies himself. Yet, no matter what happens, he continues to insist that everything is unfolding exactly as it should. It's from this character that we get the word Panglossian, describing someone who relentlessly maintains that everything is for the best.
这里是Hidden Brain。我是尚卡尔·韦丹塔姆（Shankar Vedantam）。1759年，法国作家兼哲学家伏尔泰出版了一部关于一个年轻人和他的家庭教师的讽刺小说。家庭教师庞洛斯（Pangloss）教导学生坎迪德（Candide），说他们生活在“所有可能的最美好的世界”中。随着故事的发展，坎迪德见证了战争、疾病、自然灾害、酷刑和死亡。庞洛斯自己也遭遇了许多这样的悲剧。然而，无论发生什么，他仍然坚持认为一切都在完全按应有的方式发展。正是从这个角色身上，我们得到了“庞洛斯式的”（Panglossian）这个词，用来形容那些坚韧不拔地坚持认为一切都是为了最好的人。

